21. Example Customer Call
Here is an example of how the AI should function.

AI: "Thank you for calling Hyperion Auto Repair. This is Sarah. How can I help you today?"

Customer: "Hey, I have a 2020 BMW 330i and my check engine light just came on."

AI: "I can help you get that checked out. Is the light flashing or staying on steadily?"

Customer: "It's staying on."

AI: "Thanks for confirming. Would you like me to check our availability for a diagnostic appointment?"

Customer: "Yeah, can I come in tomorrow morning?"

AI: "Let me check tomorrow morning's availability."

The AI checks the actual appointment calendar.

AI: "We have an opening at 9:30 AM. Would that work for you?"

Customer: "Yeah, that's perfect."

AI: "Great. Can I get your first and last name and the best email address for your confirmation?"

Customer: "Michael Johnson. My email is michael@example.com."

AI: "Thank you. Let me confirm your appointment."

The system saves the appointment successfully.

AI: "You're confirmed for tomorrow at 9:30 AM for a check engine light diagnostic on your 2020 BMW 330i. I've sent your confirmation email. Is there anything else I can help you with?"

Customer: "No, that's it."

AI: "Perfect. We look forward to seeing you tomorrow. Have a great day!"

The system should automatically:

Save the customer information.

Save the vehicle information.

Create the appointment.

Send the confirmation email.

Save the call recording and transcript, subject to recording consent.

Generate a call summary.

Update the business dashboard.

Update appointment analytics.

The AI should also recognize urgent safety concerns. For example, a flashing check engine light or a severe brake problem should trigger an appropriate safety-oriented response rather than a routine booking script.

22. Testing & Acceptance Criteria
Before launch, I want the developer to demonstrate that the system can reliably complete the following scenarios:

Answer a call forwarded from a shop's existing number.

Understand customers speaking naturally rather than following a script.

Collect accurate vehicle and contact details.

Interpret dates such as "tomorrow," "next Monday," and "the Friday after next."

Check real calendar availability.

Book an appointment without creating a duplicate.

Send a valid confirmation email.

Transfer a customer to a live employee.

Create a callback task when nobody answers.

Correctly handle a customer who changes their mind mid-conversation.

Recover gracefully when a customer provides unclear information.

Save recordings, transcripts, and summaries.

Display accurate dashboard statistics.

Apply custom shop-specific instructions.

Prevent one shop from accessing another shop's information.

We should test calls with background noise, different accents, interruptions, automotive terminology, and real customer questions.

I also want performance testing for response latency, call failures, appointment booking accuracy, and estimated infrastructure cost per call minute.

